// src/user/aml/aml_region.pdx — R30.M3-002 (#1062)
//
// The SystemMemory address-space handler, and the point at which this
// subsystem stops being pure computation over a byte buffer.
//
// =====================================================================
// THE MAPPING / ACCESS SPLIT — and why the corpus can be honest
// =====================================================================
// Every earlier module in src/user/aml/ could be tested exhaustively
// because it touched nothing but its own arenas. A region handler
// cannot claim that: its whole purpose is to read and write addresses
// that live outside the process.
//
// So the module is cut in two, along the line the security argument
// already runs along:
//
//   THE MAPPING STEP — `aml_region_bind`. Takes a capability window
//   {win_base, win_len} and the host address that window is mapped at,
//   checks the firmware-declared region against it, and records a
//   binding. This step decides WHETHER an address may be touched and
//   WHERE it lives. It performs no access.
//
//   THE ACCESS STEP — `aml_region_read_unit` / `aml_region_write_unit`
//   and the field machinery above them. Takes a BINDING INDEX and a
//   byte offset. It has no way to name an address: the base it uses is
//   the one the mapping step recorded, and the length it clips to is the
//   one the mapping step validated.
//
// The corpus therefore exercises the REAL access step — the real bounds
// arithmetic, the real access-width selection, the real read-modify-
// write — against a synthetic backing buffer, by handing the mapping
// step a `host_va` that points at that buffer instead of at mapped
// device memory. Not one line of the code under test differs between
// the two cases. No fixture in this repository ever names a real
// physical address, and none needs to: the part that would differ is the
// part that does no arithmetic.
//
// =====================================================================
// WHY A MISSING CAPABILITY CANNOT BE BYPASSED
// =====================================================================
// The threat is concrete. A DSDT is vendor-supplied, unsigned, and can
// declare `OperationRegion(FOO, SystemMemory, <any 64-bit address>,
// <any length>)`. Nothing in the AML language stops that address from
// naming kernel text, the page tables, another driver's DMA buffer, or
// this process's own object arena.
//
// Four properties, together, make "forgot to check the capability" not
// expressible here:
//
//   1. THE ACCESS STEP TAKES NO ADDRESS. `aml_region_read_unit(b, off,
//      n)` computes `host_base(b) + off`. There is no entry point in
//      this module that accepts a raw pointer to read or write. A caller
//      that has not been through `aml_region_bind` has nothing to pass.
//
//   2. `host_base` HAS EXACTLY ONE WRITER. Only `aml_region_bind` ever
//      stores to a row, and it refuses before storing if the capability
//      handle is zero, if the window length is zero, if the host address
//      is zero, or if the declared range is not contained in the window.
//      There is no "bind unchecked" variant, because the checked one is
//      the only one.
//
//   3. THE STORAGE IS CONFINED. `aml_region_tab` and
//      `aml_region_state` are asserted by tools/verify-aml-parser.sh
//      (via `objdump -r`) to be relocated against from aml_region.o and
//      no other object. A module that reached the table directly could
//      fabricate a row; none can.
//
//   4. THE LIVENESS TEST IS THE LOOKUP. Every access path begins with
//      `aml_region_row_live`, which requires a non-zero node AND a
//      non-zero capability handle AND a non-zero host base. A row that
//      failed any gate in `aml_region_bind` was never written, so it
//      fails the liveness test as a free row does, and the access is
//      refused with AML_ERR_REGION_UNBOUND rather than proceeding
//      against zeroes.
//
// The kernel half of the argument is in src/kernel/core/cap/
// kind_op_region.pdx: there is no kernel path that yields a
// KIND_OP_REGION covering a range the caller did not already hold, and
// the window this module is handed comes out of that capability's own
// row — never out of the firmware table.
//
// REFUSAL, NEVER TRUNCATION. A declared region that exceeds its backing
// capability is refused at BIND time with AML_ERR_REGION_NOT_COVERED.
// It is not clipped to the covered part. Clipping would let a table
// declare a huge region, observe which offsets succeed, and thereby map
// out exactly what the supervisor holds — an address oracle that never
// has to be told "no". Refusal leaks one bit; truncation leaks the
// boundary.
//
// A region declared with no covering capability at all — bind called
// with a zero handle or a zero-length window — is AML_ERR_REGION_NO_CAP.
// That is the headline refusal of this milestone and it happens before
// any address is formed.
//
// =====================================================================
// ACCESS WIDTH IS DECLARED, NOT CHOSEN
// =====================================================================
// An AML `Field` declaration carries an AccessType — ByteAcc, WordAcc,
// DWordAcc, QWordAcc, AnyAcc — and it is not a hint. Real hardware
// registers latch on the transaction width: servicing a DWordAcc field
// with four byte reads can return four snapshots of a register that
// changed between them, and servicing a ByteAcc field with a dword write
// can touch three neighbouring registers with side effects. The
// declaration exists because the table author knows something the
// interpreter does not.
//
// So `aml_region_acc_bits` resolves the declared type to the exact
// transaction width used for EVERY unit access of that field:
//
//   ByteAcc  -> 8      WordAcc  -> 16
//   DWordAcc -> 32     QWordAcc -> 64
//   AnyAcc   -> 8      BufferAcc -> refused
//
// AnyAcc resolves to 8 for SystemMemory. AnyAcc means "the interpreter
// may choose", and choosing the NARROWEST is always inside the
// permission granted — a byte access to system memory is always legal,
// where a dword access to a field near the end of a region may not fit.
// The resolver is a table rather than a branch precisely so that
// R30.M3-004's PCI_Config handler, where dword is the natural unit and
// AnyAcc must resolve to 32, becomes a row here instead of a special
// case somewhere else.
//
// BufferAcc is refused with AML_ERR_REGION_ACCESS_WIDTH: it is the
// SMBus / GenericSerialBus / IPMI protocol form, where the "access" is a
// bus transaction carrying a buffer, and it has no meaning over a memory
// window. Silently treating it as ByteAcc would run an SMBus protocol
// field as raw memory.
//
// =====================================================================
// BIT-GRANULAR FIELDS AND READ-MODIFY-WRITE
// =====================================================================
// Fields are bit-granular and need not be aligned to anything. A
// DWordAcc field at bit offset 3 with width 40 spans two dwords, and
// both halves must be assembled from ALIGNED dword transactions at the
// declared width — never from a narrower access that happens to land
// where the bits are.
//
// The read loop walks the field's bit extent one access unit at a time:
// for each unit it computes the intersection of the field with that
// unit, reads the WHOLE unit at the declared width, extracts the
// intersecting bits, and appends them to the result at the running
// output bit position.
//
// The write loop is the same walk with the UpdateRule (§19.5.5.2)
// deciding what happens to the bits of a touched unit the field does not
// cover:
//
//   Preserve (0)      read the unit, replace only the field's bits,
//                     write it back. This is the read-modify-write, and
//                     it is the default.
//   WriteAsOnes (1)   uncovered bits become 1; NO read is performed.
//   WriteAsZeros (2)  uncovered bits become 0; NO read is performed.
//
// The suppressed read under rules 1 and 2 is deliberate and is the
// reason those rules exist: they name registers where reading has side
// effects (clear-on-read status bits, FIFO pops). An implementation that
// read anyway "to be safe" would corrupt exactly the hardware the rule
// was written to protect.
//
// =====================================================================
// STRADDLING IS AN ERROR, NOT A NARROWER ACCESS
// =====================================================================
// If an access unit at the declared width would extend past the end of
// the region, the access is refused with AML_ERR_REGION_OOB. It is not
// wrapped, not partially completed, and not silently narrowed to the
// bytes that fit.
//
// Narrowing would be the tempting repair — the bits the field wants are
// inside the region, after all. It is wrong twice: it changes the
// transaction width the table declared (see above), and it means the
// region's length no longer bounds what a table can reach with a
// well-chosen field offset. A field that straddles the end of its region
// is a table bug, and reporting it at the access is the only place with
// enough context to say which field.
//
// =====================================================================
// THE FIELDUNIT BOUNDARY MOVES HERE
// =====================================================================
// R30.M2-004 (#1057) left every FieldUnit load and store refused with
// AML_ERR_BAD_TARGET (41), with the note that "writing one is a bus
// transaction and belongs to R30.M3's region handlers". This issue is
// that milestone, and the boundary moves.
//
// NOW REAL: a load or store of a FieldUnit whose enclosing `Field`
// names a SystemMemory OperationRegion that has been BOUND to a
// capability window. Reads assemble from aligned units at the declared
// width; writes read-modify-write under the UpdateRule.
//
// THE NEW BOUNDARY, stated so the next issue inherits a line and not a
// guess:
//
//   * SystemIO, PCI_Config, EmbeddedControl and every other space are
//     AML_ERR_REGION_SPACE (62). They are #1063 / #1064 / #1065, and
//     each is a row in `aml_region_space_supported`, not a branch
//     somewhere else.
//   * A FieldUnit under an IndexField or a BankField is
//     AML_ERR_REGION_INDIRECT_FIELD (64). Those are two-step protocols
//     — write an index register, then access a data register — and the
//     step is a real access with its own ordering requirements. Faking
//     it as a direct offset would silently read the wrong register.
//   * A field wider than 64 bits is AML_ERR_REGION_FIELD_WIDTH (61).
//     ACPI says such a field reads as a Buffer, not an Integer; the
//     Buffer-valued form needs the object model's heap and is R30.M4.
//     Truncating to 64 bits would return a plausible wrong answer.
//   * A FieldUnit whose region was never bound is
//     AML_ERR_REGION_UNBOUND (58) — the refusal that catches "the
//     supervisor never obtained a capability for this region".
//   * AML_ERR_BAD_TARGET (41) keeps its remaining meaning: a store
//     destination that names no cell at all.
//
// =====================================================================
// FUEL
// =====================================================================
// Every unit access spends one unit of evaluator fuel, via the same
// `aml_eval_spend` the interpreter's termination guarantee rests on. A
// field spanning K access units costs K, not 1: the cost model has to
// track the work, or a `While` loop over a wide unaligned field becomes
// a way to buy unbounded hardware transactions with a bounded fuel
// budget. `aml_region_accesses` counts the same events so the corpus can
// assert the two agree.
//
// =====================================================================
// STORAGE
// =====================================================================
// 16 rows of 8 u64s. Row 0 is the null sentinel — a binding index of 0
// means "no binding", the same convention the arenas use, which is what
// lets `aml_region_find` return a bare index with no separate presence
// flag. 15 usable bindings; a real DSDT declares on the order of a dozen
// OperationRegions. Exhaustion is a clean latched
// AML_ERR_REGION_TABLE_FULL (63), never an overwrite.

module AmlRegion = structure {

  // ==========================================================================
  // Error codes — continuing the shared code space (#1054 §E). A code
  // still identifies its origin unambiguously.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let AML_ERR_REGION_NO_CAP         : u64 = 56
  pub let AML_ERR_REGION_NOT_COVERED    : u64 = 57
  pub let AML_ERR_REGION_UNBOUND        : u64 = 58
  pub let AML_ERR_REGION_OOB            : u64 = 59
  pub let AML_ERR_REGION_ACCESS_WIDTH   : u64 = 60
  pub let AML_ERR_REGION_FIELD_WIDTH    : u64 = 61
  pub let AML_ERR_REGION_SPACE          : u64 = 62
  pub let AML_ERR_REGION_TABLE_FULL     : u64 = 63
  pub let AML_ERR_REGION_INDIRECT_FIELD : u64 = 64

  // ==========================================================================
  // Address spaces this handler services. One row per space; #1063,
  // #1064 and #1065 each flip one.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let AML_SPACE_SYSTEM_MEMORY : u64 = 0x00
  pub let AML_SPACE_SYSTEM_IO     : u64 = 0x01
  pub let AML_SPACE_PCI_CONFIG    : u64 = 0x02
  pub let AML_SPACE_EC            : u64 = 0x03

  pub let AML_REGION_MAX  : u64 = 16
  pub let AML_REGION_WORDS: u64 = 8

  // ==========================================================================
  // Storage — CONFINED TO THIS OBJECT (see the header, property 3).
  //
  // Row layout, `aml_region_tab[b*8 + k]`:
  //   [0] node        OPREGION arena node; 0 = free row
  //   [1] space       RegionSpaceKeyword
  //   [2] decl_base   the address the TABLE asked for. Recorded for
  //                   reporting only; never used to form an address.
  //   [3] decl_len    length in bytes; the clip every access uses
  //   [4] host_base   host address of decl_base. THE ONLY ADDRESS THE
  //                   ACCESS STEP TOUCHES.
  //   [5] cap_handle  non-zero token of the KIND_OP_REGION the window
  //                   came from. Zero is impossible in a live row.
  //   [6] win_base    the capability window's base
  //   [7] win_len     the capability window's length
  //
  // aml_region_state: [0] live bindings, [1] unit accesses, [2] refusals.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let mut aml_region_tab   : [u64; 128] = uninit @align(8)
  pub let mut aml_region_state : [u64; 8]   = uninit @align(8)

  // ==========================================================================
  // aml_region_reset() — drop every binding.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let aml_region_reset : () -> () !{mem} @{} =
    fn () -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M3-002 (#1062): zero all 16 rows and the state block. Bulk-zeroing here rather than lazily is not tidiness: a stale row is a recorded permission to touch a host address, and a fresh session that found one would access memory the previous session's capability justified. Row 0 is zeroed with the rest and stays the null sentinel. Reserved labels: amr_rs_.",
      block: {
        lea rcx, [rip + aml_region_tab];
        xor rax, rax;
        xor r8, r8;
      amr_rs_loop:
        cmp r8, 128;
        jae amr_rs_state;
        mov [rcx], rax;
        add rcx, 8;
        add r8, 1;
        jmp amr_rs_loop;
      amr_rs_state:
        lea rcx, [rip + aml_region_state];
        mov [rcx + 0], rax;
        mov [rcx + 8], rax;
        mov [rcx + 16], rax;
        mov [rcx + 24], rax;
        mov [rcx + 32], rax;
        mov [rcx + 40], rax;
        mov [rcx + 48], rax;
        mov [rcx + 56], rax;
        ret
      }
    }

  pub let aml_region_count : () -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn () -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M3-002 (#1062): number of live bindings. Leaf accessor. Reserved labels: none.",
      block: {
        lea rcx, [rip + aml_region_state];
        mov rax, [rcx + 0];
        ret
      }
    } @no_frame

  pub let aml_region_accesses : () -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn () -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M3-002 (#1062): total unit accesses performed since reset. Exists so the corpus can assert the fuel spend and the access count agree -- a handler that spent fuel once per FIELD rather than once per ACCESS UNIT would pass every value assertion and still under-price a wide unaligned field. Leaf accessor. Reserved labels: none.",
      block: {
        lea rcx, [rip + aml_region_state];
        mov rax, [rcx + 8];
        ret
      }
    } @no_frame

  pub let aml_region_refusals : () -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn () -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M3-002 (#1062): count of binds refused. A refusal is a security event -- a table asked for a window nobody granted -- and counting it separately from the latched error code means a fixture can prove a refusal HAPPENED even when a later success overwrites the latch. Leaf accessor. Reserved labels: none.",
      block: {
        lea rcx, [rip + aml_region_state];
        mov rax, [rcx + 16];
        ret
      }
    } @no_frame

  // ==========================================================================
  // aml_region_space_supported(space) -> u64
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let aml_region_space_supported : (u64) -> u64 !{} @{} =
    fn (space: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M3-002 (#1062): 1 iff this milestone services the space. Today exactly one row: SystemMemory (0). SystemIO (#1063), PCI_Config (#1064) and EmbeddedControl (#1065) each add a row here and a width-resolution row in aml_region_acc_bits -- which is the whole reason the predicate is a function rather than a `cmp` inside bind. A table that declares an unserviced space is REFUSED (AML_ERR_REGION_SPACE) rather than serviced as memory: treating an EC region as system memory would dereference the EC's port numbers as physical addresses. Pure leaf. Reserved labels: amr_sp_.",
      block: {
        cmp rdi, 0;                      // AML_SPACE_SYSTEM_MEMORY
        je amr_sp_yes;
        xor rax, rax;
        ret;
      amr_sp_yes:
        mov rax, 1;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // aml_region_mask(n) -> u64 — low-n-bits mask, correct at n == 64.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let aml_region_mask : (u64) -> u64 !{} @{} =
    fn (n: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M3-002 (#1062): (1 << n) - 1 for n in 0..64. The n >= 64 case is handled explicitly and is not pedantry: x86 masks the shift count to 6 bits, so `shl rax, 64` is a NO-OP and the naive form yields 1-1 = 0 -- a full-width field would then read and write ZERO bits, and the failure is silent because every value it produces is a plausible 0. n == 0 yields 0, which is the right answer and is never reached by the field loops (they compute n >= 1 by construction). Pure leaf. Reserved labels: amr_mk_.",
      block: {
        cmp rdi, 64;
        jae amr_mk_all;
        cmp rdi, 0;
        je amr_mk_none;
        mov rcx, rdi;
        mov rax, 1;
        shl rax, cl;
        sub rax, 1;
        ret;
      amr_mk_all:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF;
        ret;
      amr_mk_none:
        xor rax, rax;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // aml_region_contains(w_base, w_len, d_base, d_len) -> u64
  //
  // Overflow-safe containment — the userspace mirror of the kernel's
  // opregion_range_contains, and deliberately the same shape.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let aml_region_contains : (u64, u64, u64, u64) -> u64 !{} @{} =
    fn (w_base: u64) (w_len: u64) (d_base: u64) (d_len: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M3-002 (#1062): 1 iff [d_base, d_base+d_len) is inside [w_base, w_base+w_len). NOT ONE COMPARISON ADDS TWO CALLER-SUPPLIED VALUES, because d_base and d_len both come from a firmware table and `d_base + d_len` wraps for d_base = 2^64-1, d_len = 2 -- reporting CONTAINED for a region that starts at the top of the address space and runs off the end of it. Gates: w_len == 0 -> 0; d_len == 0 -> 0 (a zero-length region is not a region, and admitting one would let a table register an arbitrary address and read it back out of the binding, an address oracle needing no access); d_base < w_base -> 0; d_len > w_len -> 0 (checked before the subtraction below so it cannot underflow); (d_base - w_base) > (w_len - d_len) -> 0. Both subtractions are proven non-underflowing by the gates above them. This duplicates the kernel's opregion_range_contains deliberately: the two live on opposite sides of a privilege boundary and neither may depend on the other having run. Pure leaf. Reserved labels: amr_ct_.",
      block: {
        cmp rsi, 0;
        je amr_ct_no;
        cmp rcx, 0;
        je amr_ct_no;
        cmp rdx, rdi;
        jb amr_ct_no;
        cmp rcx, rsi;
        ja amr_ct_no;

        mov r10, rdx;
        sub r10, rdi;                    // d_base - w_base
        mov r11, rsi;
        sub r11, rcx;                    // w_len - d_len
        cmp r10, r11;
        ja amr_ct_no;

        mov rax, 1;
        ret;

      amr_ct_no:
        xor rax, rax;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // Row accessors. `b` is a binding index in 1..AML_REGION_MAX-1.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let aml_region_row_live : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (b: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M3-002 (#1062): 1 iff b names a live binding. THIS IS THE GATE EVERY ACCESS PATH OPENS WITH, and it asks three questions rather than one: b in 1..15 (index 0 is the null sentinel), node != 0, cap_handle != 0, host_base != 0. A row that failed any check in aml_region_bind was never written, so it fails here exactly as a free row does -- which is what makes 'the bind was refused' and 'there was never a bind' the same, safe outcome at the access site instead of two code paths one of which could be forgotten. Leaf; pure read. Reserved labels: amr_lv_.",
      block: {
        cmp rdi, 1;
        jb amr_lv_no;
        cmp rdi, 16;                     // AML_REGION_MAX
        jae amr_lv_no;

        mov r8, rdi;
        shl r8, 6;                       // b * 8 words * 8 bytes
        lea r9, [rip + aml_region_tab];
        add r8, r9;

        mov rax, [r8 + 0];               // node
        cmp rax, 0;
        je amr_lv_no;
        mov rax, [r8 + 40];              // cap_handle
        cmp rax, 0;
        je amr_lv_no;
        mov rax, [r8 + 32];              // host_base
        cmp rax, 0;
        je amr_lv_no;

        mov rax, 1;
        ret;

      amr_lv_no:
        xor rax, rax;
        ret
      }
    }

  pub let aml_region_row_word : (u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (b: u64) (w: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M3-002 (#1062): word w of row b, 0 for a dead row or an out-of-range word. The accessors below are written in terms of this one so the bounds check exists once. Leaf; pure read. Reserved labels: amr_rw_.",
      block: {
        cmp rsi, 8;
        jae amr_rw_no;
        cmp rdi, 1;
        jb amr_rw_no;
        cmp rdi, 16;
        jae amr_rw_no;

        mov r8, rdi;
        shl r8, 6;
        mov r9, rsi;
        shl r9, 3;
        add r8, r9;
        lea r9, [rip + aml_region_tab];
        add r8, r9;
        mov rax, [r8 + 0];
        ret;

      amr_rw_no:
        xor rax, rax;
        ret
      }
    }

  pub let aml_region_node : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (b: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M3-002 (#1062): the OPREGION arena node a binding covers. Reserved labels: none.",
      block: {
        xor rsi, rsi;
        call aml_region_row_word;
        ret
      }
    }

  pub let aml_region_space : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (b: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M3-002 (#1062): the address space of a binding. Reserved labels: none.",
      block: {
        mov rsi, 1;
        call aml_region_row_word;
        ret
      }
    }

  pub let aml_region_base : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (b: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M3-002 (#1062): the DECLARED base of a binding -- the address the firmware table asked for. Exposed for reporting and for the corpus; the access step never reads it, which is the point. Reserved labels: none.",
      block: {
        mov rsi, 2;
        call aml_region_row_word;
        ret
      }
    }

  pub let aml_region_len : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (b: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M3-002 (#1062): the declared length of a binding, in bytes. This is the clip every access is measured against. Reserved labels: none.",
      block: {
        mov rsi, 3;
        call aml_region_row_word;
        ret
      }
    }

  pub let aml_region_cap : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (b: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M3-002 (#1062): the capability handle the binding was derived from. Non-zero in every live row by construction. Reserved labels: none.",
      block: {
        mov rsi, 5;
        call aml_region_row_word;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // aml_region_find(node) -> b | 0
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let aml_region_find : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (node: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M3-002 (#1062): the binding index for an OPREGION node, 0 if unbound. Linear scan over 15 rows; the table is small and the alternative -- caching the index in the arena node -- would mutate the parse tree, which #1057 established must stay byte-identical across an evaluation so it remains valid as the IPC wire format. A zero node argument answers 0 rather than matching the free rows, which is what stops a caller that lost track of its node from binding onto the first free slot. Leaf; pure read. Reserved labels: amr_fd_.",
      block: {
        cmp rdi, 0;
        je amr_fd_no;

        mov r8, 1;
      amr_fd_loop:
        cmp r8, 16;
        jae amr_fd_no;

        mov r9, r8;
        shl r9, 6;
        lea r10, [rip + aml_region_tab];
        add r9, r10;
        mov rax, [r9 + 0];
        cmp rax, rdi;
        je amr_fd_hit;
        add r8, 1;
        jmp amr_fd_loop;

      amr_fd_hit:
        mov rax, r8;
        ret;

      amr_fd_no:
        xor rax, rax;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // aml_region_bind(node, cap_handle, win_base, win_len, host_va) -> b | 0
  //
  // THE MAPPING STEP. The only writer of a row, and the only place a
  // host address enters this module.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let aml_region_bind : (u64, u64, u64, u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (node: u64) (cap_handle: u64) (win_base: u64)
       (win_len: u64) (host_va: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M3-002 (#1062): bind a firmware-declared OperationRegion to a capability window. THE DECLARED RANGE COMES FROM THE PARSE TREE (the untrusted side: node arg0/arg1 index the u64 side table) AND THE WINDOW COMES FROM THE CAPABILITY (the trusted side, read out of the KIND_OP_REGION row by the caller) -- the two are never the same argument, which is what makes the containment check meaningful rather than a comparison of a value with itself. Gates in order: (1) node is a live OPREGION (kind 10) else AML_ERR_REGION_UNBOUND. (2) cap_handle == 0, win_len == 0 or host_va == 0 -> AML_ERR_REGION_NO_CAP. THIS IS THE HEADLINE REFUSAL: a region the supervisor holds no capability for is refused before any address is formed, and the refusal counter is bumped so a fixture can prove it happened. (3) space unsupported -> AML_ERR_REGION_SPACE. (4) declared length zero -> AML_ERR_REGION_NOT_COVERED. (5) aml_region_contains(win_base, win_len, decl_base, decl_len) == 0 -> AML_ERR_REGION_NOT_COVERED, WITH NO ROW WRITTEN AND NO CLIPPING -- clipping would turn the refusal into an address oracle. (6) already bound -> return the existing index rather than a second row, so a re-bind cannot produce two rows for one region with different windows. (7) free row scan from index 1 (0 is the null sentinel); exhaustion -> AML_ERR_REGION_TABLE_FULL. host_base = host_va + (decl_base - win_base), the one address the access step will ever use; the subtraction cannot underflow because gate 5 proved decl_base >= win_base. Registers r12=node, r13=cap_handle, r14=decl_base, r15=decl_len; win_base/win_len/host_va spilled. Reserved labels: amr_bd_.",
      block: {
        push r12;
        push r13;
        push r14;
        push r15;
        sub rsp, 48;

        mov r12, rdi;                    // node
        mov r13, rsi;                    // cap_handle
        mov [rsp + 0], rdx;              // win_base
        mov [rsp + 8], rcx;              // win_len
        mov [rsp + 16], r8;              // host_va

        cmp r12, 0;
        je amr_bd_unbound;

        mov rdi, r12;
        call aml_node_kind;
        cmp rax, 10;                     // AML_NODE_OPREGION
        jne amr_bd_unbound;

        // -- Gate 2: is there a capability at all?
        cmp r13, 0;
        je amr_bd_no_cap;
        mov rax, [rsp + 8];
        cmp rax, 0;
        je amr_bd_no_cap;
        mov rax, [rsp + 16];
        cmp rax, 0;
        je amr_bd_no_cap;

        // -- Gate 3: space.
        mov rdi, r12;
        call aml_node_flags;
        mov [rsp + 24], rax;             // space
        mov rdi, rax;
        call aml_region_space_supported;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je amr_bd_bad_space;

        // -- Declared base / length, out of the u64 side table.
        mov rdi, r12;
        call aml_node_arg0;
        mov rdi, rax;
        call aml_u64_get;
        mov r14, rax;                    // decl_base

        mov rdi, r12;
        call aml_node_arg1;
        mov rdi, rax;
        call aml_u64_get;
        mov r15, rax;                    // decl_len

        cmp r15, 0;
        je amr_bd_not_cov;

        // -- Gate 5: CONTAINMENT.
        mov rdi, [rsp + 0];              // win_base
        mov rsi, [rsp + 8];              // win_len
        mov rdx, r14;                    // decl_base
        mov rcx, r15;                    // decl_len
        call aml_region_contains;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je amr_bd_not_cov;

        // -- Gate 6: already bound?
        mov rdi, r12;
        call aml_region_find;
        cmp rax, 0;
        jne amr_bd_out;

        // -- Gate 7: free row.
        mov r8, 1;
      amr_bd_scan:
        cmp r8, 16;
        jae amr_bd_full;
        mov r9, r8;
        shl r9, 6;
        lea r10, [rip + aml_region_tab];
        add r9, r10;
        mov rax, [r9 + 0];
        cmp rax, 0;
        je amr_bd_found;
        add r8, 1;
        jmp amr_bd_scan;

      amr_bd_found:
        mov [r9 + 0], r12;               // node
        mov rax, [rsp + 24];
        mov [r9 + 8], rax;               // space
        mov [r9 + 16], r14;              // decl_base
        mov [r9 + 24], r15;              // decl_len

        // host_base = host_va + (decl_base - win_base)
        mov rax, r14;
        mov r10, [rsp + 0];
        sub rax, r10;                    // offset into the window
        mov r10, [rsp + 16];
        add rax, r10;
        mov [r9 + 32], rax;              // host_base

        mov [r9 + 40], r13;              // cap_handle
        mov rax, [rsp + 0];
        mov [r9 + 48], rax;              // win_base
        mov rax, [rsp + 8];
        mov [r9 + 56], rax;              // win_len

        lea r10, [rip + aml_region_state];
        mov rax, [r10 + 0];
        add rax, 1;
        mov [r10 + 0], rax;              // live bindings

        mov rax, r8;                     // binding index
        jmp amr_bd_out;

      amr_bd_no_cap:
        mov rdi, 56;                     // AML_ERR_REGION_NO_CAP
        jmp amr_bd_refuse;
      amr_bd_not_cov:
        mov rdi, 57;                     // AML_ERR_REGION_NOT_COVERED
        jmp amr_bd_refuse;
      amr_bd_bad_space:
        mov rdi, 62;                     // AML_ERR_REGION_SPACE
        jmp amr_bd_refuse;
      amr_bd_full:
        mov rdi, 63;                     // AML_ERR_REGION_TABLE_FULL
        jmp amr_bd_refuse;
      amr_bd_unbound:
        mov rdi, 58;                     // AML_ERR_REGION_UNBOUND

      amr_bd_refuse:
        call aml_eval_set_err;
        lea r10, [rip + aml_region_state];
        mov rax, [r10 + 16];
        add rax, 1;
        mov [r10 + 16], rax;             // refusals
        xor rax, rax;

      amr_bd_out:
        add rsp, 48;
        pop r15;
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // aml_region_bounds_ok(b, off, nbytes) -> u64
  //
  // THE CLIP. An access unit must lie entirely inside the region.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let aml_region_bounds_ok : (u64, u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (b: u64) (off: u64) (nbytes: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M3-002 (#1062): 1 iff [off, off+nbytes) lies entirely inside the binding's declared length; otherwise 0, having latched AML_ERR_REGION_OOB. Overflow-safe: len < nbytes -> refuse, then off > len - nbytes -> refuse, so `off + nbytes` is never formed and an off near 2^64 cannot wrap into range. A STRADDLING ACCESS IS REFUSED, NOT NARROWED: the bytes that fit are not read, nothing is partially completed, and the declared transaction width is not silently reduced -- see the module header for why narrowing is the wrong repair. A dead binding is refused with AML_ERR_REGION_UNBOUND, so a caller cannot reach the address arithmetic with an index it never bound. Reserved labels: amr_bo_.",
      block: {
        push r12;
        push r13;
        push r14;
        push r15;

        mov r12, rdi;
        mov r13, rsi;
        mov r14, rdx;

        call aml_region_row_live;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je amr_bo_unbound;

        cmp r14, 0;
        je amr_bo_oob;                   // a zero-width access is a bug

        mov rdi, r12;
        call aml_region_len;
        mov r15, rax;                    // decl_len

        cmp r15, r14;
        jb amr_bo_oob;                   // region shorter than one unit

        mov r10, r15;
        sub r10, r14;                    // len - nbytes
        cmp r13, r10;
        ja amr_bo_oob;

        mov rax, 1;
        pop r15;
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        ret;

      amr_bo_oob:
        mov rdi, 59;                     // AML_ERR_REGION_OOB
        jmp amr_bo_fail;
      amr_bo_unbound:
        mov rdi, 58;                     // AML_ERR_REGION_UNBOUND
      amr_bo_fail:
        call aml_eval_set_err;
        xor rax, rax;
        pop r15;
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // aml_region_read_unit(b, off, nbytes) -> value
  //
  // THE ACCESS STEP. Takes no address.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let aml_region_read_unit : (u64, u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (b: u64) (off: u64) (nbytes: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M3-002 (#1062): read one access unit of 1, 2, 4 or 8 bytes at byte offset `off` within binding `b`. Bounds are checked FIRST, before any address is formed, which is what lets the corpus place its synthetic backing buffer flush against a PROT_NONE guard page: a missing clip is a SIGSEGV, not a silently tolerated over-read of the next fixture. The address is host_base + off, and host_base is the value aml_region_bind computed from the CAPABILITY window -- the declared base is never added in here, so a table that lied about its address cannot steer the load. One fuel unit is spent per unit access via aml_eval_spend, so a field spanning K units costs K; the access counter moves with it. An nbytes that is not 1/2/4/8 is refused as AML_ERR_REGION_ACCESS_WIDTH rather than approximated. Returns 0 on any refusal, with the code latched -- callers in the field loops call aml_region_bounds_ok first precisely because a legitimate unit value may also be 0. Reserved labels: amr_ru_.",
      block: {
        push r12;
        push r13;
        push r14;
        push r15;

        mov r12, rdi;
        mov r13, rsi;
        mov r14, rdx;

        mov rdi, r12;
        mov rsi, r13;
        mov rdx, r14;
        call aml_region_bounds_ok;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je amr_ru_zero;

        call aml_eval_spend;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je amr_ru_zero;                  // fuel exhausted; already latched

        lea r10, [rip + aml_region_state];
        mov rax, [r10 + 8];
        add rax, 1;
        mov [r10 + 8], rax;

        mov rdi, r12;
        mov rsi, 4;                      // host_base
        call aml_region_row_word;
        add rax, r13;                    // + off
        mov r15, rax;                    // the one address

        cmp r14, 1;
        je amr_ru_b;
        cmp r14, 2;
        je amr_ru_w;
        cmp r14, 4;
        je amr_ru_d;
        cmp r14, 8;
        je amr_ru_q;

        mov rdi, 60;                     // AML_ERR_REGION_ACCESS_WIDTH
        call aml_eval_set_err;
        jmp amr_ru_zero;

        // The three narrow loads are each preceded by an explicit
        // zeroing of the destination. mov_b and mov_w write only the low
        // 8 / 16 bits of the register and LEAVE THE REST -- so without
        // this, a byte field would come back with whatever the address
        // computation left in the upper 56 bits, which is a plausible
        // enormous number rather than an obvious failure.
      amr_ru_b:
        xor rax, rax;
        mov_b rax, [r15 + 0];
        jmp amr_ru_out;
      amr_ru_w:
        xor rax, rax;
        mov_w rax, [r15 + 0];
        jmp amr_ru_out;
      amr_ru_d:
        xor rax, rax;
        mov_d rax, [r15 + 0];
        jmp amr_ru_out;
      amr_ru_q:
        mov rax, [r15 + 0];
        jmp amr_ru_out;

      amr_ru_zero:
        xor rax, rax;

      amr_ru_out:
        pop r15;
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // aml_region_write_unit(b, off, nbytes, value) -> 1 | 0
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let aml_region_write_unit : (u64, u64, u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (b: u64) (off: u64) (nbytes: u64) (value: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M3-002 (#1062): write one access unit of 1, 2, 4 or 8 bytes. Same clip-before-address discipline and the same single source of the address as the read. The store is SIZED (mov_b / mov_w / mov_d / plain mov): a 64-bit store used to write a byte field would clobber the seven following bytes, and those bytes are neighbouring hardware registers -- this is the single most consequential encoder choice in the file, which is why the widths are four explicit branches rather than a computed store. One fuel unit per unit access, matching the read. Reserved labels: amr_wu_.",
      block: {
        push r12;
        push r13;
        push r14;
        push r15;

        mov r12, rdi;
        mov r13, rsi;
        mov r14, rdx;
        mov r15, rcx;                    // value

        mov rdi, r12;
        mov rsi, r13;
        mov rdx, r14;
        call aml_region_bounds_ok;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je amr_wu_no;

        call aml_eval_spend;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je amr_wu_no;

        lea r10, [rip + aml_region_state];
        mov rax, [r10 + 8];
        add rax, 1;
        mov [r10 + 8], rax;

        mov rdi, r12;
        mov rsi, 4;
        call aml_region_row_word;
        add rax, r13;
        mov r10, rax;                    // the one address

        cmp r14, 1;
        je amr_wu_b;
        cmp r14, 2;
        je amr_wu_w;
        cmp r14, 4;
        je amr_wu_d;
        cmp r14, 8;
        je amr_wu_q;

        mov rdi, 60;                     // AML_ERR_REGION_ACCESS_WIDTH
        call aml_eval_set_err;
        jmp amr_wu_no;

      amr_wu_b:
        mov_b [r10 + 0], r15;
        jmp amr_wu_ok;
      amr_wu_w:
        mov_w [r10 + 0], r15;
        jmp amr_wu_ok;
      amr_wu_d:
        mov_d [r10 + 0], r15;
        jmp amr_wu_ok;
      amr_wu_q:
        mov [r10 + 0], r15;

      amr_wu_ok:
        mov rax, 1;
        pop r15;
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        ret;

      amr_wu_no:
        xor rax, rax;
        pop r15;
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // aml_region_acc_bits(field_elem_node) -> 8 | 16 | 32 | 64 | 0
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let aml_region_acc_bits : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (node: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M3-002 (#1062): resolve the DECLARED AccessType of a field element to the transaction width every unit access of that field must use. ByteAcc 1 -> 8, WordAcc 2 -> 16, DWordAcc 3 -> 32, QWordAcc 4 -> 64. AnyAcc 0 -> 8 for a memory window: AnyAcc delegates the choice to the interpreter, and the narrowest choice is always inside the permission granted, where a wider one may not fit near the end of a region. BufferAcc 5 and every other value -> 0, refused by the caller as AML_ERR_REGION_ACCESS_WIDTH; BufferAcc is the SMBus / GenericSerialBus / IPMI protocol form whose access carries a buffer over a bus, and running it as raw memory would execute a protocol field as an address. A TABLE, not a branch, because #1064's PCI_Config handler must resolve AnyAcc to 32 -- that becomes a row here rather than a special case elsewhere. Reserved labels: amr_ab_.",
      block: {
        call aml_field_access_type;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je amr_ab_8;
        cmp rax, 1;
        je amr_ab_8;
        cmp rax, 2;
        je amr_ab_16;
        cmp rax, 3;
        je amr_ab_32;
        cmp rax, 4;
        je amr_ab_64;
        xor rax, rax;
        ret;
      amr_ab_8:
        mov rax, 8;
        ret;
      amr_ab_16:
        mov rax, 16;
        ret;
      amr_ab_32:
        mov rax, 32;
        ret;
      amr_ab_64:
        mov rax, 64;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // aml_region_acc_log(aw_bits) -> 3 | 4 | 5 | 6 | 0
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let aml_region_acc_log : (u64) -> u64 !{} @{} =
    fn (aw: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M3-002 (#1062): log2 of the access width in bits. The field loops divide a bit offset by the access width on every iteration; every legal width is a power of two, so the division is a shift, and computing the shift ONCE outside the loop rather than dividing inside it keeps the loop free of the one operation whose cost varies with its operands. Returns 0 for a width this module does not admit, which the callers have already refused. Pure leaf. Reserved labels: amr_al_.",
      block: {
        cmp rdi, 8;
        je amr_al_3;
        cmp rdi, 16;
        je amr_al_4;
        cmp rdi, 32;
        je amr_al_5;
        cmp rdi, 64;
        je amr_al_6;
        xor rax, rax;
        ret;
      amr_al_3:
        mov rax, 3;
        ret;
      amr_al_4:
        mov rax, 4;
        ret;
      amr_al_5:
        mov rax, 5;
        ret;
      amr_al_6:
        mov rax, 6;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // aml_region_of_field(field_elem_node) -> opregion node | 0
  //
  // Walks a FIELD_ELEM up to its enclosing Field declaration and
  // resolves the region that declaration names.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let aml_region_of_field : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (node: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M3-002 (#1062): the OPREGION node a field element belongs to, 0 with a latched code otherwise. A FIELD_ELEM's parent is its FIELD declaration, and it is the FIELD that carries the REGION's NameString -- #1054 established that FIELD/INDEXFIELD/BANKFIELD are name-TRANSPARENT for exactly this reason, so the name on the parent is the region's and not the field's. The name is resolved from the scope CONTAINING the Field declaration (the FIELD node's own parent), because that is the scope the region name was written in. INDEXFIELD (12) and BANKFIELD (13) are refused with AML_ERR_REGION_INDIRECT_FIELD (64): they name a two-step protocol -- write an index or bank register, then access a data register -- and treating their elements as direct offsets into a region would read a completely different register while looking entirely successful. That refusal is the honest statement of where this milestone stops. A parent that is not a field declaration at all is AML_ERR_REGION_UNBOUND. Locals r12=node, r13=field decl. Reserved labels: amr_of_.",
      block: {
        push r12;
        push r13;
        push r14;
        push r15;

        mov r12, rdi;
        cmp r12, 0;
        je amr_of_unbound;

        mov rdi, r12;
        call aml_node_kind;
        cmp rax, 14;                     // AML_NODE_FIELD_ELEM
        jne amr_of_unbound;

        mov rdi, r12;
        call aml_node_parent;
        mov r13, rax;
        cmp r13, 0;
        je amr_of_unbound;

        mov rdi, r13;
        call aml_node_kind;
        cmp rax, 12;                     // INDEXFIELD
        je amr_of_indirect;
        cmp rax, 13;                     // BANKFIELD
        je amr_of_indirect;
        cmp rax, 11;                     // FIELD
        jne amr_of_unbound;

        // Scope containing the Field declaration.
        mov rdi, r13;
        call aml_node_parent;
        mov r14, rax;

        mov rdi, r13;
        call aml_node_name;
        mov r15, rax;
        cmp r15, 0;
        je amr_of_unbound;

        mov rdi, r14;
        mov rsi, r15;
        call aml_eval_resolve;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je amr_of_fail;                  // aml_eval_resolve latched already
        mov r14, rax;

        mov rdi, r14;
        call aml_node_kind;
        cmp rax, 10;                     // OPREGION
        jne amr_of_unbound;

        mov rax, r14;
        pop r15;
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        ret;

      amr_of_indirect:
        mov rdi, 64;                     // AML_ERR_REGION_INDIRECT_FIELD
        jmp amr_of_latch;
      amr_of_unbound:
        mov rdi, 58;                     // AML_ERR_REGION_UNBOUND
      amr_of_latch:
        call aml_eval_set_err;
      amr_of_fail:
        xor rax, rax;
        pop r15;
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // aml_region_field_binding(field_elem_node) -> b | 0
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let aml_region_field_binding : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (node: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M3-002 (#1062): the binding a field element resolves through, 0 with a latched code otherwise. Chains aml_region_of_field then aml_region_find then aml_region_row_live. THE LIVENESS CHECK IS NOT REDUNDANT WITH THE FIND: find matches on the node alone, and the only rows whose node is non-zero are rows aml_region_bind wrote -- but stating the liveness requirement HERE, at the single point every access path passes through, is what makes 'a region that was never granted a capability' impossible to reach the arithmetic through, rather than merely unlikely to. An unbound region is AML_ERR_REGION_UNBOUND (58), which is the code that means 'the supervisor holds no capability covering this region'. Reserved labels: amr_fb_.",
      block: {
        push r12;
        push r13;
        push r14;
        push r15;

        call aml_region_of_field;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je amr_fb_fail;                  // already latched

        mov rdi, rax;
        call aml_region_find;
        mov r12, rax;
        cmp r12, 0;
        je amr_fb_unbound;

        mov rdi, r12;
        call aml_region_row_live;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je amr_fb_unbound;

        mov rax, r12;
        pop r15;
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        ret;

      amr_fb_unbound:
        mov rdi, 58;                     // AML_ERR_REGION_UNBOUND
        call aml_eval_set_err;
      amr_fb_fail:
        xor rax, rax;
        pop r15;
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // aml_region_field_read(field_elem_node) -> u64
  //
  // Assemble a bit-granular, possibly unaligned field from aligned
  // access units at the DECLARED width.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let aml_region_field_read : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (node: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M3-002 (#1062): read a FieldUnit. Refusals first: a field of zero width or wider than 64 bits is AML_ERR_REGION_FIELD_WIDTH (61) -- ACPI makes a >64-bit field read as a BUFFER, and truncating it to 64 bits would return a plausible wrong answer, so it is refused until the Buffer-valued form lands; a binding that does not exist is AML_ERR_REGION_UNBOUND; an access type this space cannot service is AML_ERR_REGION_ACCESS_WIDTH. The walk: cur = bit_offset, end = bit_offset + bit_width; each iteration takes the unit containing `cur`, computes lo = cur - unit_base_bit and n = min(access_width - lo, end - cur), READS THE WHOLE UNIT AT THE DECLARED WIDTH (never a narrower access that happens to cover the bits), extracts bits [lo, lo+n) and appends them at the running output position. A unit whose aligned extent leaves the region is refused by aml_region_bounds_ok as AML_ERR_REGION_OOB -- straddling is an error, not a narrower access. Frame: [rsp+0] cur, +8 end, +16 out, +24 outbit, +32 lo, +40 n, +48 unit_base_bit, +56 mask. r12=node, r13=binding, r14=access width in bits, r15=log2 of it. Reserved labels: amr_fr_.",
      block: {
        push r12;
        push r13;
        push r14;
        push r15;
        sub rsp, 64;

        mov r12, rdi;

        // -- Field extent.
        mov rdi, r12;
        call aml_node_arg0;
        mov [rsp + 0], rax;              // cur = bit offset
        mov r10, rax;

        mov rdi, r12;
        call aml_node_arg1;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je amr_fr_badw;
        cmp rax, 64;
        ja amr_fr_badw;
        mov r11, [rsp + 0];
        add rax, r11;
        mov [rsp + 8], rax;              // end

        // -- Binding.
        mov rdi, r12;
        call aml_region_field_binding;
        mov r13, rax;
        cmp r13, 0;
        je amr_fr_fail;

        // -- Declared access width.
        mov rdi, r12;
        call aml_region_acc_bits;
        mov r14, rax;
        cmp r14, 0;
        je amr_fr_badacc;
        mov rdi, r14;
        call aml_region_acc_log;
        mov r15, rax;

        xor rax, rax;
        mov [rsp + 16], rax;             // out
        mov [rsp + 24], rax;             // outbit

      amr_fr_loop:
        mov rax, [rsp + 0];
        mov r10, [rsp + 8];
        cmp rax, r10;
        jae amr_fr_done;

        // unit_base_bit = (cur >> log) << log
        mov r11, rax;
        mov rcx, r15;
        shr r11, cl;
        shl r11, cl;
        mov [rsp + 48], r11;

        // lo = cur - unit_base_bit
        mov r8, rax;
        sub r8, r11;
        mov [rsp + 32], r8;

        // n = min(aw - lo, end - cur)
        mov r9, r14;
        sub r9, r8;
        mov r10, [rsp + 8];
        sub r10, rax;
        cmp r9, r10;
        jbe amr_fr_n_ok;
        mov r9, r10;
      amr_fr_n_ok:
        mov [rsp + 40], r9;

        mov rdi, r9;
        call aml_region_mask;
        mov [rsp + 56], rax;

        // Bounds-check the ALIGNED unit before reading it.
        mov rdi, r13;
        mov rsi, [rsp + 48];
        shr rsi, 3;                      // unit byte offset
        mov rdx, r14;
        shr rdx, 3;                      // unit byte width
        call aml_region_bounds_ok;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je amr_fr_fail;

        mov rdi, r13;
        mov rsi, [rsp + 48];
        shr rsi, 3;
        mov rdx, r14;
        shr rdx, 3;
        call aml_region_read_unit;

        mov rcx, [rsp + 32];             // lo
        shr rax, cl;
        mov r10, [rsp + 56];             // mask
        and rax, r10;
        mov rcx, [rsp + 24];             // outbit
        shl rax, cl;
        mov r10, [rsp + 16];
        or  r10, rax;
        mov [rsp + 16], r10;

        mov rax, [rsp + 24];
        mov r10, [rsp + 40];
        add rax, r10;
        mov [rsp + 24], rax;             // outbit += n

        mov rax, [rsp + 0];
        add rax, r10;
        mov [rsp + 0], rax;              // cur += n
        jmp amr_fr_loop;

      amr_fr_done:
        mov rax, [rsp + 16];
        add rsp, 64;
        pop r15;
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        ret;

      amr_fr_badw:
        mov rdi, 61;                     // AML_ERR_REGION_FIELD_WIDTH
        jmp amr_fr_latch;
      amr_fr_badacc:
        mov rdi, 60;                     // AML_ERR_REGION_ACCESS_WIDTH
      amr_fr_latch:
        call aml_eval_set_err;
      amr_fr_fail:
        xor rax, rax;
        add rsp, 64;
        pop r15;
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // aml_region_field_store(field_elem_node, value) -> 1 | 0
  //
  // The write half, with the UpdateRule deciding the fate of the
  // neighbouring bits.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let aml_region_field_store : (u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (node: u64) (value: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M3-002 (#1062): write a FieldUnit. Same refusals and the same unit walk as the read. Per unit: m = mask(n) << lo is the field's footprint inside this unit and newbits = ((value >> outbit) & mask(n)) << lo are the bits going in. THE UPDATERULE (§19.5.5.2) THEN DECIDES THE REST OF THE UNIT. Preserve (0) READS THE UNIT AND MERGES: (unit & ~m) | newbits -- this is the read-modify-write, and it is what stops a 3-bit field at bit 5 of a byte from clearing the other five bits, which on real hardware are five other control bits. WriteAsOnes (1) writes (~m) | newbits and WriteAsZeros (2) writes newbits, and CRUCIALLY NEITHER READS FIRST: those rules exist precisely to name registers where reading has a side effect -- clear-on-read status, FIFO pop -- and an implementation that read anyway to be safe would corrupt the hardware the rule was written to protect. Frame: [rsp+0] cur, +8 end, +16 value, +24 outbit, +32 lo, +40 n, +48 unit_base_bit, +56 mask, +64 m, +72 newbits, +80 update rule. r12=node, r13=binding, r14=access width bits, r15=log2. Reserved labels: amr_fw_.",
      block: {
        push r12;
        push r13;
        push r14;
        push r15;
        sub rsp, 96;

        mov r12, rdi;
        mov [rsp + 16], rsi;             // value

        mov rdi, r12;
        call aml_node_arg0;
        mov [rsp + 0], rax;              // cur

        mov rdi, r12;
        call aml_node_arg1;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je amr_fw_badw;
        cmp rax, 64;
        ja amr_fw_badw;
        mov r11, [rsp + 0];
        add rax, r11;
        mov [rsp + 8], rax;              // end

        mov rdi, r12;
        call aml_region_field_binding;
        mov r13, rax;
        cmp r13, 0;
        je amr_fw_fail;

        mov rdi, r12;
        call aml_region_acc_bits;
        mov r14, rax;
        cmp r14, 0;
        je amr_fw_badacc;
        mov rdi, r14;
        call aml_region_acc_log;
        mov r15, rax;

        mov rdi, r12;
        call aml_field_update_rule;
        mov [rsp + 80], rax;

        xor rax, rax;
        mov [rsp + 24], rax;             // outbit

      amr_fw_loop:
        mov rax, [rsp + 0];
        mov r10, [rsp + 8];
        cmp rax, r10;
        jae amr_fw_done;

        mov r11, rax;
        mov rcx, r15;
        shr r11, cl;
        shl r11, cl;
        mov [rsp + 48], r11;             // unit_base_bit

        mov r8, rax;
        sub r8, r11;
        mov [rsp + 32], r8;              // lo

        mov r9, r14;
        sub r9, r8;
        mov r10, [rsp + 8];
        sub r10, rax;
        cmp r9, r10;
        jbe amr_fw_n_ok;
        mov r9, r10;
      amr_fw_n_ok:
        mov [rsp + 40], r9;              // n

        mov rdi, r9;
        call aml_region_mask;
        mov [rsp + 56], rax;             // mask

        // m = mask << lo
        mov rcx, [rsp + 32];
        shl rax, cl;
        mov [rsp + 64], rax;

        // newbits = ((value >> outbit) & mask) << lo
        mov rax, [rsp + 16];
        mov rcx, [rsp + 24];
        shr rax, cl;
        mov r10, [rsp + 56];
        and rax, r10;
        mov rcx, [rsp + 32];
        shl rax, cl;
        mov [rsp + 72], rax;

        // Bounds-check the aligned unit before touching it.
        mov rdi, r13;
        mov rsi, [rsp + 48];
        shr rsi, 3;
        mov rdx, r14;
        shr rdx, 3;
        call aml_region_bounds_ok;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je amr_fw_fail;

        // Fast path: the field covers the WHOLE unit, so no rule applies
        // and no read is needed under any of the three.
        mov rax, [rsp + 40];
        cmp rax, r14;
        je amr_fw_whole;

        mov rax, [rsp + 80];
        cmp rax, 1;
        je amr_fw_ones;
        cmp rax, 2;
        je amr_fw_zeros;

        // -- Preserve: READ-MODIFY-WRITE.
        mov rdi, r13;
        mov rsi, [rsp + 48];
        shr rsi, 3;
        mov rdx, r14;
        shr rdx, 3;
        call aml_region_read_unit;
        mov r10, [rsp + 64];             // m
        mov r11, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF;
        xor r10, r11;                    // ~m
        and rax, r10;                    // keep the neighbours
        mov r10, [rsp + 72];
        or  rax, r10;                    // insert the field
        jmp amr_fw_put;

      amr_fw_ones:
        mov r10, [rsp + 64];
        mov r11, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF;
        xor r10, r11;                    // ~m
        mov rax, r10;
        mov r10, [rsp + 72];
        or  rax, r10;
        jmp amr_fw_put;

      amr_fw_zeros:
        mov rax, [rsp + 72];
        jmp amr_fw_put;

      amr_fw_whole:
        mov rax, [rsp + 72];

      amr_fw_put:
        mov rdi, r13;
        mov rsi, [rsp + 48];
        shr rsi, 3;
        mov rdx, r14;
        shr rdx, 3;
        mov rcx, rax;
        call aml_region_write_unit;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je amr_fw_fail;

        mov rax, [rsp + 24];
        mov r10, [rsp + 40];
        add rax, r10;
        mov [rsp + 24], rax;

        mov rax, [rsp + 0];
        add rax, r10;
        mov [rsp + 0], rax;
        jmp amr_fw_loop;

      amr_fw_done:
        mov rax, 1;
        add rsp, 96;
        pop r15;
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        ret;

      amr_fw_badw:
        mov rdi, 61;                     // AML_ERR_REGION_FIELD_WIDTH
        jmp amr_fw_latch;
      amr_fw_badacc:
        mov rdi, 60;                     // AML_ERR_REGION_ACCESS_WIDTH
      amr_fw_latch:
        call aml_eval_set_err;
      amr_fw_fail:
        xor rax, rax;
        add rsp, 96;
        pop r15;
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // aml_region_field_load_obj(field_elem_node) -> object | 0
  //
  // The object-model entry point the evaluator's read path calls.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let aml_region_field_load_obj : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (node: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M3-002 (#1062): read a FieldUnit and wrap the result as an Integer object, 0 with a latched code on any refusal. The wrap is a separate function from aml_region_field_read because the read's zero is a LEGITIMATE VALUE and the object path's zero is a failure: distinguishing them here, once, by consulting aml_eval_err rather than the returned value, is what stops a field that genuinely reads 0 from being reported as a refusal at every call site. A field at most 64 bits wide is by definition an Integer per ACPI; the Buffer-valued form for wider fields is refused upstream. Reserved labels: amr_lo_.",
      block: {
        push r12;
        push r13;
        push r14;
        push r15;

        call aml_eval_err;
        mov r13, rax;                    // error state on entry

        call aml_region_field_read;
        mov r12, rax;

        call aml_eval_err;
        cmp rax, r13;
        jne amr_lo_fail;                 // a NEW error was latched

        mov rdi, r12;
        call aml_obj_int;
        pop r15;
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        ret;

      amr_lo_fail:
        xor rax, rax;
        pop r15;
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // aml_region_handles(op16) -> u64
  //
  // The executable statement of what this module services, asserted by
  // the corpus in both directions — the same device as
  // aml_arith_handles / aml_ref_handles / aml_ctl_handles.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let aml_region_handles : (u64) -> u64 !{} @{} =
    fn (op16: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M3-002 (#1062): this module handles NO OPCODE. It is reached through the FieldUnit destination and source paths in aml_eval, not through an opcode dispatch, and saying so as an executable predicate is worth more than leaving the question open -- the sibling modules all answer this question and a reader comparing them would otherwise have to infer the answer from absence. When R30.M4 adds an explicit region-access operator this becomes a row. Pure leaf. Reserved labels: none.",
      block: {
        xor rax, rax;
        ret
      }
    }

}
